item.text:
Reused evidence: none.

New evidence:
- `USDOS baseline`: the 2022 State Department report places most formal power in the PM’s office and says Bangladesh had credible reports of unlawful killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary detention, restrictions on expression and assembly, and abuses against minorities and women, with weak accountability.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"US Department of State","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089131.html","claim":"The report says the prime minister's office concentrates most power and lists unlawful killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary detention, free-expression and assembly restrictions, political-participation limits, and abuses against minorities and women as significant 2022 problems with poor accountability.","locator":"executive summary lines 36-39","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government human-rights report summarizing the full year with explicit Bangladesh-specific findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers calendar year 2022","ruler_attribution":"Institutional baseline under Hasina's government, not proof of a specific personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says civilian authorities maintained effective control over the security forces, which narrows attribution to policy and oversight failures rather than total collapse."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.7","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- `HRW crackdown`: Human Rights Watch says 2022 saw escalating repression against the BNP, mass arrests and raids, ruling-party attacks on opposition meetings, and at least four deaths and hundreds of injuries in clashes.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Crackdown on Political Opposition","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/10/bangladesh-crackdown-political-opposition","claim":"HRW said opposition groups were reporting escalation of repression, including mass arrests and police raids of opposition homes, and said at least four people reportedly died and hundreds were injured in clashes involving police, BNP supporters, and Awami League supporters after late-August protests.","locator":"lines 607-615","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights-organization reporting with concrete event counts and direct attribution questions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government and its aligned actors as described in the report, though each side disputed blame for the violence.","contrary_evidence":["The report notes each side accused the other of instigating violence."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
- `Amnesty demonstrations`: Amnesty said the December 2022 BNP clashes showed excessive force, at least one death, more than 60 injuries, live ammunition, and a mass-arrest drive reaching about 1,400 activists.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Authorities must end repression of demonstrations","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/12/bangladesh-authorities-must-end-repression-of-demonstrations/","claim":"Amnesty said the clash in Dhaka involved at least one death and more than 60 injuries, that police used live ammunition, pellets, rubber bullets, and tear gas, and that the authorities had launched mass arrests of political activists reaching about 1,400 people.","locator":"lines 61-73","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights-organization statement with specific injury and arrest details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Government and police conduct under Hasina's administration; the source does not isolate a single command order.","contrary_evidence":["The article is a rights-group response to media reports, not a state audit of every casualty."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
- `OHCHR protests`: UN special procedures recorded allegations that BNP protestors were attacked with excessive and lethal force in the July-September 2022 price-rise protests, with at least four deaths.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Communication report and search","publisher":"OHCHR","publication_date":"2022-12-27","url":"https://spcommreports.ohchr.org/TmSearch/Mandates?m=44","claim":"The UN communications record says it received allegations of attacks and excessive and lethal force, including by law enforcement, against BNP protestors during July-September 2022 demonstrations over price rises, with at least four deaths and injuries reported.","locator":"BGD 5/2022 entry in the Mandates page","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN human-rights communications record, though it summarizes allegations rather than adjudicated findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Bangladeshi authorities under Hasina, but the page records allegations received rather than a final finding.","contrary_evidence":["This is an allegation record, not a judicial or investigatory conclusion."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}
- `Prothom Alo clash`: local reporting on 7 December 2022 says the BNP-police clashes left one dead, more than 50 injured, and several hundred activists detained after a raid on the BNP office.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"BNP-police clashes leave one dead as Naya Paltan turns battleground","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-12-07","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/politics/mmdh40an9z","claim":"The clashes outside BNP headquarters left one person dead and more than 50 BNP supporters injured, and police then raided the party office and detained several hundred activists.","locator":"article lead and body summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local reporting with named event details and arrest counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"The event occurred under Hasina's government and involved police and ruling-party context, though the article also records BNP claims and counterclaims.","contrary_evidence":["BNP leaders said police instigated the violence and sought to thwart the rally."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}
- `DSA editors council`: Prothom Alo reports the Editors’ Council said the Digital Security Act was obstructing media freedom and had been used against journalists, activists, writers, and artists.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Digital Security Act hinders freedom of media: Editor’s Council","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-05-06","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/amp/story/bangladesh/digital-security-act-hinders-freedom-of-media-editors-council","claim":"The Editors' Council said the Digital Security Act was obstructing media freedom and that cases under it had been filed against journalists and other critics including activists, writers, and artists.","locator":"search result snippet / article summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local professional-body criticism reported by a major Bangladeshi outlet; the underlying point is direct but the accessible text is a summary snippet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"The critique is directed at a law used under Hasina's government; it does not by itself prove her personal intent in each case.","contrary_evidence":["The government had said it would amend the law, showing some acknowledgment of the criticism."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8"]}
- `HRW CHT death`: Human Rights Watch says soldiers detained and beat Indigenous activist Nabayan Chakma Milon in March 2022, that he died in custody, and that this fit a broader pattern of military abuse in the Chittagong Hill Tracts.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Indigenous Activist Dies in Military Custody","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-30","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/30/bangladesh-indigenous-activist-dies-military-custody","claim":"HRW said soldiers detained Milon in Dighinala, beat and kicked him for over an hour, took him away in a military vehicle, and that he was later pronounced dead in hospital; HRW said this fit a broader pattern of military abuses in the CHT.","locator":"lines 593-597","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed contemporaneous rights reporting with corroborating regional-commission testimony and specific location/time details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Bangladesh military forces under Hasina's government, though the report does not identify a direct PM order.","contrary_evidence":["The source is advocacy reporting, but it is corroborated by an independent CHT Commission account."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.7","3B.10"]}
- `OHCHR CHT death`: OHCHR communications records allegations of torture, ill-treatment, and the death of Nabayan Chakma Milon in military custody in the Chittagong Hill Tracts.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Communication report and search","publisher":"OHCHR","publication_date":"2022-08-10","url":"https://spcommreports.ohchr.org/TmSearch/Mandates?m=46","claim":"The UN communications record says it received allegations of torture, ill-treatment, and the subsequent death of Nabayan Chakma Milon, an Indigenous political activist, in military custody in the Chittagong Hill Tracts.","locator":"BGD 3/2022 entry in the Mandates page","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN communications record of a Bangladesh-specific allegation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Bangladesh military custody under Hasina's government; still an allegation record rather than adjudicated finding.","contrary_evidence":["The page records a communication, not a final legal finding."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.7"]}
- `RAB defense`: Hasina defended the Rapid Action Battalion's counterterrorism role, questioned the US sanctions, and dismissed the UN disappearance list as faulty.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Hasina questions sanctions on RAB, wonders if US is unhappy with its counter-terrorism activities","publisher":"bdnews24.com","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://bdnews24.com/bangladesh/8h1r37d6vk","claim":"Hasina defended the Rapid Action Battalion as a counterterrorism force, questioned the sanctions placed on it, suggested the US should share responsibility because it trained the unit, and said the UN list of disappearance victims was faulty.","locator":"article body lines 141-156","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous reporting with clear quotations from the prime minister.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Hasina as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["This is a defense of the force, not proof of accountability or reform; it also conflicts with later rights reporting on reprisals and impunity."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.6","3B.10"]}
- `HRW reprisals`: HRW's 2022 retrospective says that after sanctions on RAB, authorities launched threats and intimidation against human rights defenders and families of disappeared victims and increased surveillance and harassment.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Bangladesh","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/bangladesh","claim":"HRW said that after sanctions on the Rapid Action Battalion, Bangladesh authorities launched a campaign of threats and intimidation against human rights defenders and families of disappearance victims, while security forces ramped up surveillance and harassment of rights groups.","locator":"lines 66-74","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual country chapter summarizing the 2022 period with direct claims about reprisals and surveillance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers events of 2022 in a retrospective country chapter","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Hasina government and its security apparatus, though the report is retrospective and combines multiple episodes.","contrary_evidence":["The same HRW section says extrajudicial killings and enforced disappearances dropped dramatically after the US sanctions, implying the authorities could reduce abuse when pressured."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- `HRW Rohingya`: HRW says Bangladesh intensified restrictions on Rohingya livelihoods, movement, and education, including school closures, shop destruction, curfews, checkpoint harassment, and severe restrictions on Bhasan Char.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Bangladesh","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/bangladesh","claim":"HRW said Bangladesh intensified restrictions on Rohingya livelihoods, movement, and education, including closing community-led schools, destroying shops, imposing curfews and checkpoint harassment, and keeping many refugees under severe restrictions on Bhasan Char.","locator":"lines 101-105","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual country chapter summarizing 2022 with concrete treatment of a vulnerable group.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers events of 2022 in a retrospective country chapter","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Bangladesh authorities under Hasina's government, though refugee policy also reflects inherited burden and Myanmar-related constraints.","contrary_evidence":["The same section notes a joint repatriation verification plan, showing the government was also pursuing a diplomatic route even though conditions were not safe for return."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}
- `HRW women and LGBT`: HRW says women and girls still had little recourse to protection and justice, 193 women and girls were reportedly murdered by husbands or family members in 2022, and LGBT people faced violence and threats without adequate police protection.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Bangladesh","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/bangladesh","claim":"HRW said women in Bangladesh continued to have little recourse to protection, services, or justice in domestic-violence cases, that 193 women and girls were reportedly murdered by husbands or family members in 2022, and that LGBT people faced violence and threats without adequate police protection.","locator":"lines 90-96","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual country chapter summarizing 2022 with concrete vulnerable-group harm indicators and protection gaps.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers events of 2022 in a retrospective country chapter","ruler_attribution":"Mostly structural and institutional, but still attributable to the government environment Hasina oversaw.","contrary_evidence":["The same section notes a cabinet-approved Evidence Act amendment, showing some partial reform pressure."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}
- `HRW Evidence Act`: HRW notes that the cabinet approved a draft amendment removing parts of the Evidence Act that allowed defense lawyers to denigrate the character of women pursuing sexual-violence cases.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Bangladesh","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/bangladesh","claim":"HRW reported that the cabinet approved a draft amendment to section 155(4) of the Evidence Act that removed aspects allowing the defense to denigrate the character of women who pursue sexual-violence charges.","locator":"lines 92-93","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual country chapter with a concrete legal reform noted in the 2022 period.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers events of 2022 in a retrospective country chapter","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Hasina-led cabinet as a partial protective reform.","contrary_evidence":["This is a narrow procedural reform and does not erase the broader safety and accountability failures described in the same chapter."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.7"]}
- `Hasina election openness`: Hasina said she wanted all parties to contest the next parliamentary election and said there was no problem if parties stayed out of the alliance.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Hasina says she wants all parties to contest in next parliamentary election","publisher":"bdnews24.com","publication_date":"2022-09-15","url":"https://bdnews24.com/politics/kvlzhad7mk","claim":"Hasina said she wanted all parties to contest the next parliamentary election and that there was no problem if someone remained in or left the alliance.","locator":"article body lines 141-145","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous reporting with explicit quotations from the prime minister.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is permissive rhetoric, not proof that opposition parties were actually able to operate without retaliation."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.10"]}
- `Prothom Alo rally peaceful`: despite the surrounding arrests and police clashes, Prothom Alo said the BNP's Dhaka rally ended peacefully after the authorities finally allowed it to proceed.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"আন্দোলনের দ্বিতীয় ধাপে বিএনপি","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-12-10","url":"https://www.prothomalo.com/politics/xlfndnadjd","claim":"The BNP's Dhaka rally ended peacefully after last-minute permission, even though the surrounding political atmosphere had been tense and earlier clashes and arrests had already occurred.","locator":"article body lines 46-59","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local-language reporting with a clear description of the rally's eventual peaceful end.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the broader government-police environment rather than a single named order.","contrary_evidence":["The article also says the event came after prior clashes, arrests, and security fears, so it is only partial counterevidence."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}
- `Hasina anti-BNP rhetoric`: Hasina called BNP and Jamaat a group of murderers and war criminals and urged people to keep them from returning to power.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM Hasina urges people to unite against BNP-Jamaat nexus","publisher":"bdnews24.com","publication_date":"2022-12-04","url":"https://bdnews24.com/politics/4pz5q8cttr","claim":"Hasina called BNP and Jamaat a group of murderers and war criminals and urged people to unite so they never returned to power.","locator":"headline and article summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct reporting of the prime minister's public rhetoric, though accessible here as a summary snippet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Hasina as prime minister and party leader.","contrary_evidence":["This is harsh political rhetoric, but it is still rhetoric rather than an explicit call for physical violence."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.9"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I considered roughly 30 documents across US State Department, HRW, Amnesty, OHCHR communications, Prothom Alo, bdnews24, CPJ, AP/Reuters searches, and a few broader democracy and rights summaries.
- I kept the 2022 and 2022-covering items that most directly mapped to physical safety, repression, protests, custody abuse, vulnerable groups, and accountability.
- I rejected or deprioritized several duplicates of the same BNP protest week from different outlets, because they repeated the same event without adding a materially distinct claim.
- I rejected older out-of-period items such as a 2021 DSA arrest story unless they were only needed for background; the chapter target is 2022.
- I also rejected many Reuters/AP search hits that were either blocked, too generic, or did not add a ruler-attributable domestic-safety fact beyond the stronger NGO, UN, and local reporting already kept.
- Some 2023 and 2024 retrospective pages were skipped when they did not directly summarize the 2022 period; the HRW 2023 country chapter was kept only where it clearly described the 2022 events.
- Generic development, economy, and election-forecast pieces were mostly discarded unless they bore directly on protest, repression, or safety.

Remaining gaps:
- There is still no single direct document showing Hasina personally ordered any one abusive police, military, or intelligence action in 2022.
- The strongest evidence is about the environment her government presided over: recurring repression, impunity, and selective control, plus a few partial reforms.
- I still do not have a clean independent audit for the exact chain of command behind the custody death in the Chittagong Hill Tracts or the December BNP crackdown.
- The evidence on protection is mixed and limited: the Evidence Act amendment and permissive election rhetoric are real, but they are narrow compared with the recurring abuse and intimidation record.
